# Rank 1192 / CVE-2023-42276: hutool's JSONArray.add(int, Object) pads the
# backing list up to a caller-supplied index before placing the element
# (CWE-400, GHSA-rxgf-r843-g53h, issue#3286@Github). When index is at or past
# the current size, the vulnerable form loops `while (index != this.size())
# this.add(JSONNull.NULL);`, appending one JSONNull per step with no upper
# bound on index -- a single call with a large index (Integer.MAX_VALUE, or
# any value far past the array's real size) drives the loop until the JVM
# runs out of heap. ListUtil.setOrPadding in hutool-core has the identical
# shape (a for-loop from size to index calling list.add(paddingElement)) and
# the same fix, but only JSONArray is modelled here since the two are the
# same weakness once.
#
# Pool commit 9ba8f9ca5dd32441f2e0f150cb22fa178bb771d3 carries no flag; read
# its diff per Step 0's note that flags are not the only source of pool
# errors, and confirmed it is the real fix: it touches exactly
# hutool-json/src/main/java/cn/hutool/json/JSONArray.java and
# hutool-core/src/main/java/cn/hutool/core/collection/ListUtil.java, both
# adding the identical two-line guard, with a CHANGELOG entry naming
# issue#3286 by number and title ("fixed JSONArray's add() method throwing
# OutOfMemory").
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap. vyql match on a minimal pair reproducing add()
# verbatim from both revisions labels nothing at all -- no source, sink, or
# issue on either one -- and vyql resolve shows every call inside the method,
# including the recursive `this.add`/`this.set`, terminating as unresolved
# rather than hinting at a missing fact. vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 4GB
# over the real hutool checkout (1996 java files) reports zero VYQL-DOS-041
# findings anywhere.
#
# Checked for reuse first: code.UnboundedAllocationReview (VYQL-DOS-041,
# CWE_400/770) is the right concept by CWE and by its own review text --
# "review allocation sizes derived from parsed counts for per-item limits,
# aggregate consistency checks, and overflow" describes exactly this loop --
# but every language that binds it does so from a dedicated per-language
# analysis fact (C's allocation.unbounded_accumulated_count at
# c.go:2611, Rust's rustReview == "unbounded_next_await", PHP's
# phpReview == "unbounded_report_period", and so on), never from
# presenceNode's flat context bag alone; Java has no such fact and none of
# the shipped ones are close enough to reuse. Confirmed why the bag alone
# cannot express this safely rather than assuming it: the only literal names
# available in the vulnerable method are "index", "add" and "size" --
# functionally, JDK convention for List.add(int index, E element) and
# Collection.size() -- and requiring their co-occurrence
# (context.identifier contains "index", context.call contains "add",
# context.callPath contains ".size") also matches an ordinary,
# entirely-safe copy loop with the same three words:
#   for (int index = 0; index < src.size(); index++) { dest.add(src.get(index)); }
# vyql match against that copy loop (not committed, checked by hand) would
# report the same three tokens present on both the padding method and this
# unrelated one, because presenceNode context has no notion of loop
# structure and no identity between the collection whose size is compared
# and the collection being grown -- the same two facts C's dedicated
# analysis correlates for the allocation case. Widening further to catch it
# would flag any bounded iterate-and-copy loop everywhere, the precision
# trap AGENTS.md warns against; narrowing to the literal expression text
# (`index!=this.size()`) answers to this file's variable names, not to
# List's documented contract, and would not generalise past hutool.
#
# What is missing is a Java analogue of ccUnboundedAccumulatedAllocationObservations
# (c.go:2589): a fact that a loop grows a collection by repeated append calls,
# terminating only when the collection's own live size reaches a parameter,
# with no prior comparison of that parameter against a bound. That is a new
# dataflow relation (same collection identity between the size check and the
# append target, plus absence-of-guard), not a one-line frontend change.
# Entered in ENGINE-GAPS.md under "Not mechanical, do not sweep these" with
# this rank's minimal pair as the regression test. No definition change
# shipped.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions reject VYQL-DOS-041.

test "rank1192: JSONArray.add pads to a caller-supplied index with no upper bound"
  lang java
  file JSONArray.java
  reject VYQL-DOS-041
  reject_review code.UnboundedAllocationReview
  code
  ```
  public class JSONArray implements JSON, JSONGetter<Integer>, List<Object>, RandomAccess {
  	@Override
  	public void add(int index, Object element) {
  		if (index < 0) {
  			throw new JSONException("JSONArray[{}] not found.", index);
  		}
  		if (index < this.size()) {
  			InternalJSONUtil.testValidity(element);
  			this.rawList.add(index, JSONUtil.wrap(element, this.config));
  		} else {
  			while (index != this.size()) {
  				this.add(JSONNull.NULL);
  			}
  			this.set(element);
  		}
  	}
  }
  ```

test "rank1192: JSONArray.add caps padding growth before looping"
  lang java
  file JSONArray.java
  reject VYQL-DOS-041
  reject_review code.UnboundedAllocationReview
  code
  ```
  public class JSONArray implements JSON, JSONGetter<Integer>, List<Object>, RandomAccess {
  	@Override
  	public void add(int index, Object element) {
  		if (index < 0) {
  			throw new JSONException("JSONArray[{}] not found.", index);
  		}
  		if (index < this.size()) {
  			InternalJSONUtil.testValidity(element);
  			this.rawList.add(index, JSONUtil.wrap(element, this.config));
  		} else {
  			if(index > (this.size() + 1) * 2) {
  				throw new JSONException("Index is too large:", index);
  			}
  			while (index != this.size()) {
  				this.add(JSONNull.NULL);
  			}
  			this.set(element);
  		}
  	}
  }
  ```
